<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Ma'in</h1><p class="st-meta">650 BCE – 100 BCE (approx.) · capital Qarnawu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Minaean kingdom of al-Jawf, whose merchants ran the incense trade from Yemen to Egypt and even the Greek island of Delos.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ma'in arose in the al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-jawf-yemen">Jawf</a> valley north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marib">Marib</a>, in a region of city-states once subject to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba</a>. By the 6th or 5th century BCE it was independent, its kings ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarnawu">Qarnawu</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baraqish">Yathill</a> (Baraqish) as a second major city.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ma'in was above all a trading state. Minaean merchants organised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> caravans to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gaza">Gaza</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, kept a colony at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-dedan">Dedan</a> in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hejaz">Hejaz</a>, and left inscriptions as far away as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delos">Delos</a>. Inscriptions list Minaean men marrying foreign wives from Gaza and Egypt. The main god was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadd">Wadd</a>, and the kings governed with councils drawn from leading families. The language was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minaic-language">Minaic</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ma'in depended on caravan trade and lost importance as Ptolemaic and Roman shipping in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> grew. Around 100 BCE it was absorbed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba</a>. The walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baraqish">Baraqish</a> still stand in al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-jawf-yemen">Jawf</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-main.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Ma'in" width="320" height="329"><figcaption>Image: Wario2 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Ma%27in.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>650 BCE – 100 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Qarnawu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abyada' Yathi', Waqah'il Sadiq</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>South Arabian polytheism (Wadd)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Minaic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Yemen</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Minaean Kingdom, Ma'in, Minaeans</dd></dl></aside></div>
